You are an expert in TypeScript, Node.js, Vite, Vue 3, Vue Router, Element Plus, Vant, VueUse, and Tailwind.

Code Style and Structure
- Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples.
- Use composition API and declarative programming patterns; avoid options API.
- Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication.
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isLoading, hasError).
- Structure files: exported component, composables, helpers, static content, types.

Naming Conventions
- Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., components/auth-wizard).
- Use PascalCase for component names (e.g., AuthWizard.vue).
- Use camelCase for composables (e.g., useAuthState.ts).

TypeScript Usage
- Use TypeScript for all code; prefer types over interfaces.
- Avoid enums; use const objects instead.
- Use Vue 3 with TypeScript and `<script setup lang="ts">`.

Syntax and Formatting
- Use arrow functions for methods and computed properties.
- Avoid unnecessary curly braces in conditionals; use concise syntax for simple statements.
- Use template syntax for declarative rendering.

UI and Styling
- Use Element Plus (desktop), Vant (mobile), and Tailwind for components and styling.
- Implement responsive design with Tailwind CSS; use a mobile-first approach.
- Responsive breakpoint: `web: 804px` separates mobile from desktop layouts.

Performance Optimization
- Use Suspense for asynchronous components.
- Implement lazy loading for routes and components.
- Optimize images: use WebP format, include size data, implement lazy loading.
- Optimize Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, FID).

Key Conventions
- Use VueUse for common composables and utility functions.
- Use Pinia for state management.
- Path aliases: `@` and `~@` both resolve to `src/`.
- Auto-imports via unplugin-auto-import and unplugin-vue-components (Element Plus, Vant, Iconify).

Project-specific Guidelines (Vue 3 + Vite)
- Follow the project directory structure: `src/views/` (pages), `src/components/`, `src/composables/`, `src/stores/`, `src/utils/`.
- Routing is defined in `src/router/index.ts` with Vue Router; use lazy imports for route components when appropriate.
- Data fetching uses axios (`src/apis/`) and composables; no Nuxt useFetch/useAsyncData.
- Global modals are managed via `useModalStore` in `src/stores/common.ts`.
- React components (Privy auth) live in `src/react_app/` and are embedded via veaury.

Internationalization (i18n)
- All user-facing text MUST use vue-i18n (`$t()` / `t()`); never hardcode strings in templates or components.
- Locale files live in `src/lang/locales/`: `en.json`, `zh.json`, `ko.json`, `ja.json`, `es.json`, `hi.json`, `id.json`.
- When adding, modifying, or removing any i18n key or user-facing text, update ALL locale files in sync — same keys, same structure, across every language file. No language may be left behind; use English as placeholder only when a proper translation is not yet available.
- `en.json` is the fallback locale; keep key order and nesting consistent across all files.
- SEO/meta descriptions in `src/lang/index.ts` (`SEO_DESCRIPTION`) must also be updated when related copy changes.

Vue 3 and Composition API Best Practices
- Use `<script setup>` syntax for concise component definitions.
- Leverage ref, reactive, and computed for reactive state management.
- Use provide/inject for dependency injection when appropriate.
- Implement custom composables for reusable logic.

Follow the official Vue.js, Vue Router, and Vite documentation for up-to-date best practices on Data Fetching, Rendering, and Routing.
